{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 15, :comments ["<b>And he turned.</b> Scripture mentions here, 'and the mountain burned with fire' (Deuteronomy 9:15). The sense of 'and he turned' is like a person looking to see what he should do, and he saw that he was obligated to descend quickly, in the manner of 'and he turned this way and that' (above, [Exodus] 32:[24])." "<b>And the sense of 'in his hand.'</b> By his authority, like 'and you shall take in your hand ten men' — for we are astonished how he could bring down one tablet, let alone two in one hand." "And since Scripture explicitly states that they were written on both their sides, they are not the two stones from the stones mentioned in the breastpiece, for what is written on one side would show through on the other side, and the meaning of 'written' is not 'visible' but only 'written,' for as he wrote on this side, so it was written on the other side — and the tablet is thick. There is no need for the mem and samekh of the tablets to stand miraculously, for there is a dispute over the interpretation of the word אשורית: whether it is 'confirmed' in its letters, or named so because it went up with them from Assyria. Moreover, if they were visible, they would appear inverted. And it is not fitting to object against me: if these were precious stones, the answer is that they were very precious because of the inscription of Elohim, which was engraved upon them, for they needed to be thick. And where would one find a very precious stone a cubit long, a cubit and a half wide, and a cubit high? And furthermore, from where would Moses hew two tablets of precious stone like the measure of the first ones? Now, we do not know how the Ten Words were written on the two tablets without inverting them; therefore it says 'on this side and that they are written' after stating 'written on both their sides.' Nor can we know whether the entire tablet was written upon, or whether margins were left on its four sides as we do today with scrolls — nor whether the inscription was thick or thin. And a matter we have not received by tradition — there is no sense in our speculations about it."], :book :exodus, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {2 "The phrase אם היו אלו האבנים יקרות introduces an objection Ibn Ezra anticipates; the syntax is compressed and the transition to והתשובה is abrupt, but the structure (objection + answer) is rendered as-is."}, :source-witnesses [["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:39:52.097975704Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}